<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Odawara Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1590 CE – 1871 CE · capital Odawara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain guarding the Hakone pass on the Tokaido road at the former Later Hojo capital, held mostly by the Okubo family.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> from the Later Hojo in 1590, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> gave the castle to his senior retainer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okubo-tadayo">Okubo Tadayo</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okubo-tadachika">Tadachika</a> was dismissed in 1614 in a court intrigue, and the domain passed through other hands, including the Inaba family from 1632.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> controlled the approaches to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakone-checkpoint">Hakone checkpoint</a>, the key control point on the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. The Okubo returned in 1686. The eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-fuji">Mount Fuji</a> in 1707 buried much of its farmland in ash. In the early 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okubo-tadazane">Okubo Tadazane</a> employed the agrarian reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninomiya-sontoku">Ninomiya Sontoku</a> to restore ruined villages.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> wavered between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> and the new government before submitting in 1868. After the abolition of the domains in 1871 it was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanagawa-prefecture">Kanagawa Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/odawara-domain.jpg" alt="Odawara Domain" width="320" height="427"><figcaption>Image: noface · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odawara_2006-02-21_c.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1590 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Odawara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Okubo Tadayo, Inaba Masanori, Okubo Tadazane</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Odawara han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1730&amp;focus=odawara-domain" aria-label="Odawara Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8202 903 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Odawara Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8522 1159L8522 1159M8522 1159L8530 1145M8522 1159L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8522 1159h0M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8522 1159h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8522 1159h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Odawara. <a href="/atlas?year=1730&amp;focus=odawara-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
